## [upstream] complex: arccosh returns +0 on the positive real axis

Commit: d6ec47d87

`gsl_complex_arccosh()` was implemented as `arccos` followed by a
multiplication by `i`, with the sign chosen from the imaginary part of the
intermediate result.  For a real argument the intermediate is real, so
`arccos()` returns it with a zero imaginary part and multiplying by `i`
negates the real part: the result carried a **negative** zero real part,
where `acosh()` and `gsl_complex_arccosh_real()` return a positive zero.

The value was otherwise correct - only the sign of zero differed - but
`gsl_complex_arccosh_real()` exists for exactly this case, and
`gsl_complex_arccos()` already special-cases a real argument for the same
reason, so the fix delegates `GSL_IMAG(a) == 0` to
`gsl_complex_arccosh_real()`.

The existing tables in `complex/results.h` already expected `+0` for
`arccosh(1+0i)`, but the harness compares magnitudes and cannot see the
sign.  The new vectors compare against `gsl_complex_arccosh_real()` and
assert the sign of the real part.  With the change reverted the test
reports three failures (-1, 0.5 and 1, the points whose result is real and
zero); complex arguments are untouched.

Files: `complex/math.c`, `complex/test_source.c`.

See Savannah bug #47345.


## [upstream] interpolation: NaN for a point out of the 2D interpolation domain

Commit: 2b4e2f1e5

`interp2d_eval()`, the wrapper shared by every `gsl_interp2d_eval*_e()`
function, rejected an out-of-range point with `GSL_ERROR(...)` and nothing
else.  `GSL_ERROR` calls the error handler and returns the code; it does
not touch the output argument.  So on a domain error
